<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · 1823 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">The First Nowell</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Christmas carol from Cornwall in England, first printed in 1823. &quot;Nowell&quot; is an old English word for Christmas.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional (extra words by Davies Gilbert)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (usually sung in the arrangement by John Stainer, 1871)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the people of Cornwall; later English church choirs and carol singers</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Cornwall, England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>most likely early modern, possibly earlier; current form first published 1823</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; its current form first published in 1823, with extra words by Davies Gilbert)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-first-nowell">The First Nowell</a>&quot; (or &quot;Nowel&quot;), modernised as &quot;The First Noel&quot;, is a traditional English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> of Cornish origin. It most likely dates from the early modern period, although it may be older. Its current form was first published in Carols Ancient and Modern (1823) and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> and Sandys Carols (1833), collections edited by William Sandys and arranged and edited by Davies Gilbert, who also wrote extra words.</p><p>&quot;Nowell&quot; is an Early Modern English word for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>, from the French Noël, &quot;the Christmas season&quot;, and ultimately from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> natalis [dies], &quot;[day] of birth&quot;. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> to the early modern period the word was regularly used in the burden, or refrain, of carols; 15th-century examples are found in the Ritson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscript</a>, the Trinity Carol Roll and the Selden Carol Book. As a result the word also came to mean a Christmas song or carol.</p><p>The melody is unusual among English folk melodies: it consists of one musical phrase sung twice, followed by a refrain that is a variation on that phrase, and all three phrases end on the third of the scale. Writing in the Journal of the Folk-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Society in 1915, Anne Gilchrist noted that the tune was not recorded before Sandys's publication. On the evidence of a set of church gallery parts found in Westmorland, she suggested that it may have begun as a treble part to another carol, &quot;Hark, hark what news the angels bring&quot;, which was passed down by ear and later remembered as the melody rather than as a harmony. A conjectural reconstruction of that earlier version is printed in The New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> Book of Carols.</p><p>Today the carol is usually sung in a four-part hymn arrangement by the English composer John Stainer, first published in his Carols, New and Old in 1871. As with many traditional songs and carols, the words vary from book to book. The version of The New English Hymnal (1986) is the one used in Henry Ramsden Bramley and John Stainer's collection; others are Ralph Dunstan's gallery version in the Cornish Songbook (1929) and that of the Reverend Charles Lewis Hutchins in Carols Old and Carols New (1916). Variations on the tune are included in Victor Hely-Hutchinson's Carol Symphony. In the early 1930s the American folklorist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> Carpenter made sound recordings of several traditional versions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>.</p><p>The words join two parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> story. The angels' announcement to the shepherds and the shepherds' adoration are told in the second chapter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>; the Star of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a> belongs to the story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a>, the Wise Men, in the Gospel of Matthew, and does not appear in the story of the shepherds.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A carol that tells the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> story from its beginning: angels bring the news of the birth to shepherds in the fields at night, and a star leads the Wise Men to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, where they kneel and offer their gifts. It ends by calling everyone to praise God together. The refrain repeats the old word for Christmas and announces the birth of a king. The mood is calm and joyful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The first Nowell the angels did say</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Was to certain poor shepherds in fields as they lay;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In fields where they lay, keeping their sheep,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On a cold winter's night that was so deep:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Nowell, Nowell, Nowell, Nowell,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Born is the King of Israel.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>They lookèd up and saw a star,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shining in the east, beyond them far:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And to the earth it gave great light,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And so it continued both day and night:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And by the light of that same star,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Three Wise Men came from country far;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To seek for a King was their intent,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And to follow the star whersoever it went:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>This star drew nigh to the north-west;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O'er Bethlehem it took its rest;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And there it did both stop and stay</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Right over the place where Jesus lay:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then entered in those Wise Men three,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Fell reverently upon their knee,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And offered there in his presence,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Their gold and myrrh and frankincense:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then let us all with one accord</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Sing praises to our heavenly Lord</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That hath made heaven and earth of nought,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And with his blood mankind hath bought:</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional; as English Wikisource gives it (the wording of Bramley and Stainer, 1871). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_First_Nowell" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
