<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The First Nowell (John Stainer)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1871</span><span class="sg-chain-h">four-part hymn arrangement, the usual one today</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Carol Symphony (Victor Hely-Hutchinson)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations on its theme</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hark, hark what news the angels bring</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune may have begun as a treble part to this carol (Anne Gilchrist's suggestion, 1915)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gospel of Luke, chapter 2</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the story of the angels and the shepherds</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gospel of Matthew</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the story of the Magi and the Star of Bethlehem</span></li></ul></section>
